## 1. What is HLD?

**High-Level Design (HLD)** is the architectural blueprint of a system. It describes the overall system architecture, major components/modules, data flow, technology choices, and how they interact — without going into code-level implementation details.

### Purpose of HLD

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Architecture Overview** | Shows the big picture of the entire system |
| **Component Identification** | Lists all major modules/services |
| **Data Flow** | How data moves between components |
| **Technology Stack** | Database, cache, queue, language choices |
| **Integration Points** | External systems, APIs, third-party services |
| **Non-Functional Requirements** | Scalability, availability, performance, security |

### HLD Deliverables

- Architecture diagrams (block diagrams, sequence diagrams)
- Component interaction diagrams
- Data flow diagrams
- Technology stack decisions
- Deployment architecture
- Network topology
- Capacity estimation

