## 3. System Design Framework

### Step-by-Step Approach for Interviews

#### Step 1: Requirements Clarification (3-5 min)

**Functional Requirements (FR):**
- What does the system do?
- Core features and use cases
- Who are the users?

**Non-Functional Requirements (NFR):**
- Scale (users, data volume, requests/sec)
- Availability (99.9%, 99.99%)
- Latency (< 200ms, < 50ms)
- Consistency vs Availability trade-off
- Durability (data must never be lost)

**Example — Design Twitter:**
```
FR:
- Post tweets (text, images, video)
- Follow/unfollow users
- News feed (timeline)
- Search tweets

NFR:
- 500M users, 200M DAU
- 400M tweets/day
- Read-heavy (1000:1 read-write ratio)
- Timeline generation < 200ms
- 99.99% availability
```

#### Step 2: Capacity Estimation (Back-of-the-Envelope) (3-5 min)

**Key Numbers to Remember:**

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| 1 day | 86,400 seconds ≈ 100K seconds |
| 1 million requests/day | ~12 requests/sec |
| 1 billion requests/day | ~12,000 requests/sec |
| 1 char | 1 byte (ASCII) / 2 bytes (Unicode) |
| 1 int | 4 bytes |
| 1 long | 8 bytes |
| UUID | 16 bytes |
| 1 KB | 1,024 bytes |
| 1 MB | 1,024 KB |
| 1 GB | 1,024 MB |
| 1 TB | 1,024 GB |

**Latency Numbers Every Developer Should Know:**

| Operation | Time |
|-----------|------|
| L1 cache reference | 0.5 ns |
| L2 cache reference | 7 ns |
| Main memory reference | 100 ns |
| SSD random read | 150 μs |
| HDD random read | 10 ms |
| Send 1 KB over 1 Gbps network | 10 μs |
| Read 1 MB from memory | 250 μs |
| Read 1 MB from SSD | 1 ms |
| Read 1 MB from HDD | 20 ms |
| Round trip within same datacenter | 500 μs |
| Round trip cross-continent | 150 ms |

**Estimation Template:**
```
Daily Active Users (DAU): X
Reads per user per day: Y
Writes per user per day: Z

Read QPS = DAU × Y / 86400
Write QPS = DAU × Z / 86400
Peak QPS = QPS × 2 (or 3)

Storage per record: A bytes
Daily storage = Write QPS × 86400 × A
5-year storage = Daily × 365 × 5
Bandwidth = QPS × record_size
```

#### Step 3: High-Level Design (10-15 min)

- Draw the main components
- Show APIs between components
- Data flow from client to storage and back

#### Step 4: Detailed Design (10-15 min)

- Dive deep into 2-3 critical components
- Discuss trade-offs
- Handle edge cases

#### Step 5: Bottlenecks & Improvements (5 min)

- Single points of failure
- Scale bottlenecks
- Monitoring and alerting
- Future improvements

