## 5. Scalability

### 5.1 Vertical Scaling (Scale Up)

```
Before:  [Server: 4 CPU, 8 GB RAM]
After:   [Server: 32 CPU, 128 GB RAM]
```

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Simple to implement | Hardware limits |
| No code changes | Single point of failure |
| No distributed complexity | Expensive at high end |
| ACID compliance easy | Downtime for upgrade |

### 5.2 Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out)

```
Before:  [Server 1]
After:   [Server 1] [Server 2] [Server 3] ... [Server N]
              ↑          ↑          ↑              ↑
         [=============== Load Balancer ===============]
```

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Virtually unlimited scale | Complex architecture |
| No single point of failure | Data consistency challenges |
| Cost-effective (commodity hardware) | Code must handle distribution |
| Rolling upgrades possible | Network overhead |

### 5.3 Scaling Strategies

| Strategy | Use Case |
|----------|----------|
| **Database Read Replicas** | Read-heavy workloads |
| **Database Sharding** | Write-heavy / large data |
| **Caching** | Repeated reads, computed results |
| **CDN** | Static content, global users |
| **Async Processing** | Non-blocking operations |
| **Microservices** | Independent scaling per service |
| **Auto-scaling** | Variable load patterns |

