## 6. Load Balancing

### 6.1 What is a Load Balancer?

Distributes incoming network traffic across multiple servers to ensure no single server bears too much load.

```
                    Internet
                       |
               [Load Balancer]
              /      |       \
        [Server1] [Server2] [Server3]
```

### 6.2 Types of Load Balancers

| Type | Layer | Protocol | Example |
|------|-------|----------|---------|
| **L4 (Transport)** | Layer 4 | TCP/UDP | AWS NLB, HAProxy (TCP mode) |
| **L7 (Application)** | Layer 7 | HTTP/HTTPS | AWS ALB, Nginx, HAProxy (HTTP mode) |

**L4 vs L7:**

| Feature | L4 | L7 |
|---------|----|----|
| Routing basis | IP + Port | URL, headers, cookies, content |
| Performance | Faster (less inspection) | Slightly slower |
| SSL termination | No (pass-through) | Yes |
| Content-based routing | No | Yes |
| WebSocket support | Basic | Full |

### 6.3 Load Balancing Algorithms

| Algorithm | Description | Best For |
|-----------|------------|----------|
| **Round Robin** | Rotate sequentially through servers | Equal-capacity servers |
| **Weighted Round Robin** | Like RR but with weight per server | Servers with different capacity |
| **Least Connections** | Route to server with fewest active connections | Variable request duration |
| **Weighted Least Connections** | Least connections with weights | Mixed capacity + variable load |
| **IP Hash** | Hash client IP to server | Session stickiness |
| **Least Response Time** | Route to fastest responding server | Latency-sensitive apps |
| **Random** | Randomly pick a server | Simple, stateless |
| **Consistent Hashing** | Hash-ring based distribution | Cache servers, distributed systems |

### 6.4 Health Checks

```
Load Balancer → HTTP GET /health → Server
                                     ↓
                              200 OK = Healthy
                              5xx/timeout = Unhealthy → Remove from pool
```

- **Active checks**: LB periodically pings servers
- **Passive checks**: LB monitors response codes from real traffic

### 6.5 Global Server Load Balancing (GSLB)

- DNS-based routing across data centers
- Geographic routing (serve from nearest DC)
- Disaster recovery (failover to another DC)
- Example: AWS Route 53 with latency-based or geolocation routing

