## 7. Caching

### 7.1 Why Cache?

- Reduce latency (memory access: 100ns vs disk: 10ms)
- Reduce database load
- Improve throughput
- Cost savings

### 7.2 Cache Levels

```
[Client] → [CDN Cache] → [API Gateway Cache] → [Application Cache] → [Database Cache] → [Database]
   L1           L2              L3                     L4                    L5              L6
```

| Level | Cache Type | Example |
|-------|-----------|---------|
| **Client-side** | Browser cache, mobile app cache | HTTP Cache-Control headers |
| **CDN** | Edge server cache | CloudFront, Akamai |
| **API Gateway** | Response cache at gateway | Kong, AWS API Gateway |
| **Application** | In-process / distributed cache | Redis, Memcached |
| **Database** | Query cache, buffer pool | MySQL query cache, PostgreSQL shared_buffers |

### 7.3 Caching Strategies

#### Write-Through Cache
```
App → Write to Cache → Write to DB (synchronous)
    → Read from Cache ✓
```
- **Pro**: Cache always consistent with DB
- **Con**: Higher write latency (both cache + DB)

#### Write-Behind (Write-Back) Cache
```
App → Write to Cache → Return success
                     → Async write to DB (batched)
```
- **Pro**: Low write latency
- **Con**: Data loss risk if cache fails before DB write

#### Write-Around Cache
```
App → Write directly to DB (bypass cache)
    → Read: Cache miss → Read DB → Populate cache
```
- **Pro**: Cache not polluted with infrequently read data
- **Con**: Higher latency on first read (cache miss)

#### Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)
```
Read:
  App → Check Cache → Hit? → Return data
                    → Miss? → Read DB → Write to Cache → Return data

Write:
  App → Write to DB → Invalidate Cache
```
- **Pro**: Only caches what's actually read
- **Con**: Cache miss penalty; possible stale data

#### Read-Through Cache
```
App → Read from Cache → Hit? → Return
                      → Miss? → Cache reads from DB → Return
```
- **Pro**: Simpler app code (cache handles DB reads)
- **Con**: Need cache library support

### 7.4 Cache Eviction Policies

| Policy | Description | Use Case |
|--------|------------|----------|
| **LRU (Least Recently Used)** | Evict least recently accessed item | General purpose, most common |
| **LFU (Least Frequently Used)** | Evict least frequently accessed item | Hot/cold data separation |
| **FIFO (First In First Out)** | Evict oldest item | Simple, predictable |
| **TTL (Time-To-Live)** | Evict after fixed time | Time-sensitive data |
| **Random** | Evict random item | When access patterns are uniform |

### 7.5 Cache Invalidation

| Method | Description |
|--------|------------|
| **TTL-based** | Auto-expire after set duration |
| **Event-based** | Invalidate on data change event |
| **Version-based** | Use version numbers; increment on change |
| **Purge API** | Explicit API call to invalidate |

### 7.6 Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd)

**Problem**: Many requests hit cache miss simultaneously → all query DB → overload

**Solutions:**
- **Locking**: Only one request fetches from DB; others wait
- **Pre-computation**: Refresh cache before TTL expires
- **Jittered TTL**: Add random offset to TTL to spread expiry

### 7.7 Redis vs Memcached

| Feature | Redis | Memcached |
|---------|-------|-----------|
| Data structures | Strings, Lists, Sets, Hashes, Sorted Sets, Streams | Strings only |
| Persistence | RDB + AOF | None |
| Replication | Master-Slave | None (external tools) |
| Clustering | Redis Cluster | Consistent hashing (client-side) |
| Pub/Sub | Yes | No |
| Lua scripting | Yes | No |
| Memory efficiency | Less (overhead per key) | More (simple slab allocator) |
| Multi-threaded | Single-threaded (I/O threads in v6+) | Multi-threaded |

