## 8. Database Design & Choices

### 8.1 SQL vs NoSQL

| Feature | SQL (Relational) | NoSQL |
|---------|-----------------|-------|
| **Schema** | Fixed, predefined | Flexible, dynamic |
| **Scaling** | Vertical (mostly) | Horizontal (designed for it) |
| **ACID** | Full support | Varies (often eventual consistency) |
| **Joins** | Native support | Limited/none |
| **Query Language** | SQL (standardized) | Varies by DB |
| **Best For** | Complex queries, transactions | High throughput, flexible schema |

### 8.2 Types of NoSQL Databases

| Type | Examples | Data Model | Best For |
|------|----------|-----------|----------|
| **Key-Value** | Redis, DynamoDB, Riak | Key → Value | Caching, sessions, simple lookups |
| **Document** | MongoDB, CouchDB, Firestore | JSON/BSON documents | Content management, catalogs, user profiles |
| **Column-Family** | Cassandra, HBase, ScyllaDB | Column families | Time-series, IoT, analytics |
| **Graph** | Neo4j, Amazon Neptune, ArangoDB | Nodes + Edges | Social networks, recommendations, fraud detection |
| **Time-Series** | InfluxDB, TimescaleDB, Prometheus | Timestamp + Value | Monitoring, IoT, financial data |
| **Search Engine** | Elasticsearch, Solr | Inverted index | Full-text search, log analytics |

### 8.3 Database Replication

#### Master-Slave (Primary-Replica)

```
    [Master] ───write──→ [Master DB]
       │
    replicate
       │
  ┌────┼────┐
  ↓    ↓    ↓
[Slave1][Slave2][Slave3] ←── reads
```

- Master handles all writes
- Slaves handle reads (read replicas)
- Async replication → eventual consistency
- Sync replication → strong consistency but higher latency

#### Master-Master (Multi-Master)

```
[Master1] ←──replicate──→ [Master2]
    ↕                          ↕
  reads/writes              reads/writes
```

- Both handle reads and writes
- Conflict resolution needed (last-write-wins, vector clocks)
- Higher availability
- More complex

### 8.4 ACID vs BASE

| ACID | BASE |
|------|------|
| **A**tomicity — all or nothing | **B**asically **A**vailable |
| **C**onsistency — data always valid | **S**oft state — state may change over time |
| **I**solation — concurrent txns don't interfere | **E**ventual consistency |
| **D**urability — committed data survives crashes | Prioritizes availability over consistency |
| SQL databases | NoSQL databases |

### 8.5 Database Indexing

**B-Tree Index (default in most RDBMS):**
```
              [50]
           /        \
      [20,30]      [70,80]
     /  |   \     /  |   \
  [10][25][35] [60][75][90]
```

| Index Type | Description | Use Case |
|-----------|------------|----------|
| **B-Tree** | Balanced tree, range queries | General purpose, most queries |
| **Hash** | Hash table lookup | Exact match only |
| **Bitmap** | Bit arrays | Low cardinality columns (gender, status) |
| **Full-Text** | Inverted index | Text search |
| **Composite** | Multi-column | Queries filtering on multiple columns |
| **Covering** | Includes all query columns | Avoid table lookup |
| **Partial** | Index subset of rows | Queries with WHERE clause on subset |

**When NOT to index:**
- Small tables
- Columns rarely used in WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY
- Columns with high write frequency and low read
- Columns with very low selectivity

### 8.6 Connection Pooling

```
Application → [Connection Pool (10-50 connections)] → Database
              [Conn1][Conn2]...[ConnN]  ← reused
```

- Avoids expensive connection creation per request
- Tools: HikariCP (Java), pgBouncer (PostgreSQL), ProxySQL (MySQL)

### 8.7 When to Choose What Database

| Scenario | Recommended DB | Reason |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| E-commerce transactions | PostgreSQL, MySQL | ACID, complex queries |
| User sessions | Redis | Fast key-value, TTL |
| Product catalog | MongoDB | Flexible schema |
| Social graph | Neo4j | Graph traversal |
| Real-time analytics | ClickHouse, Druid | Columnar, fast aggregation |
| IoT time-series | InfluxDB, TimescaleDB | Optimized for time data |
| Full-text search | Elasticsearch | Inverted index |
| Global distribution | CockroachDB, Spanner | Distributed SQL |
| High write throughput | Cassandra, ScyllaDB | Write-optimized, no SPOF |

