## 10. Consistent Hashing

### 10.1 Why Consistent Hashing?

**Problem with simple hashing:**
```
server = hash(key) % N
```
If N changes (add/remove server), almost ALL keys remap → massive cache miss / data redistribution.

### 10.2 How It Works

```
Hash Ring (0 to 2^32 - 1):

         S1
        / 
       /   
  S4 ●     ● S2
       \   /
        \ /
         S3

Key K1 → hash(K1) → walk clockwise → nearest server
```

**With Virtual Nodes:**
```
S1 has virtual nodes: S1_0, S1_1, S1_2 (spread across ring)
S2 has virtual nodes: S2_0, S2_1, S2_2

→ Better load distribution
→ When S1 removed, only S1's keys redistribute (to next node clockwise)
```

### 10.3 Benefits

| Benefit | Description |
|---------|------------|
| **Minimal redistribution** | Only K/N keys remapped when adding/removing node |
| **Uniform distribution** | Virtual nodes ensure even load |
| **Scalability** | Easy to add/remove nodes |
| **Used by** | Cassandra, DynamoDB, Memcached, Akamai CDN |

