## 11. CAP Theorem & PACELC

### 11.1 CAP Theorem

In a distributed system, you can guarantee only **two of three** properties:

```
        Consistency
           /\
          /  \
         /    \
        / Pick \
       /  Two   \
      /          \
     /____________\
Availability    Partition
                Tolerance
```

| Property | Definition |
|----------|-----------|
| **Consistency (C)** | Every read receives the most recent write (or an error) |
| **Availability (A)** | Every request receives a response (no guarantee it's latest) |
| **Partition Tolerance (P)** | System continues to operate despite network partitions |

**Reality**: Network partitions WILL happen → you really choose between **CP** or **AP**.

### 11.2 CAP Classifications

| Type | Behavior During Partition | Examples |
|------|--------------------------|---------|
| **CP** | Sacrifice availability for consistency | MongoDB (single-master), HBase, Redis Cluster, ZooKeeper |
| **AP** | Sacrifice consistency for availability | Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB, Riak |
| **CA** | Only possible without partitions (single node) | Traditional RDBMS (single server) |

### 11.3 PACELC Theorem

Extends CAP: Even when there is **no partition (E)**, there is a trade-off between **Latency (L)** and **Consistency (C)**.

```
If Partition → Choose A or C
Else         → Choose L or C
```

| System | PAC | ELC | Explanation |
|--------|-----|-----|-------------|
| Cassandra | PA | EL | Available during partition, low latency normally |
| MongoDB | PC | EC | Consistent during partition, consistent normally |
| DynamoDB | PA | EL | Available + low latency |
| PNUTS (Yahoo) | PC | EL | Consistent during partition, low latency normally |

