## 16. Proxy & Reverse Proxy

### 16.1 Forward Proxy

```
[Client] → [Forward Proxy] → [Internet/Server]
```

- Client knows about proxy
- Hides client identity from server
- Use cases: Bypass restrictions, caching, security, anonymity

### 16.2 Reverse Proxy

```
[Client] → [Reverse Proxy] → [Server 1]
                           → [Server 2]
                           → [Server 3]
```

- Client doesn't know about proxy
- Hides server identity from client
- Use cases: Load balancing, SSL termination, caching, compression, security

### 16.3 Reverse Proxy vs Load Balancer

| Feature | Reverse Proxy | Load Balancer |
|---------|--------------|---------------|
| Primary purpose | Proxy requests, add functionality | Distribute load |
| SSL termination | Yes | Sometimes |
| Caching | Yes | No |
| Compression | Yes | No |
| Can work with 1 server | Yes | No (needs multiple) |
| Examples | Nginx, HAProxy | AWS ELB, F5 |

> In practice, Nginx and HAProxy serve as both reverse proxy and load balancer.

