## 21. System Design Case Studies

### 21.1 Design URL Shortener (TinyURL / Bitly)

**Requirements:**
- Shorten long URLs → short URL (e.g., bit.ly/abc123)
- Redirect short URL → original URL
- Custom aliases optional
- Analytics (click count)
- High availability, low latency

**Capacity:**
- 500M new URLs/month → ~200 URLs/sec write
- 100:1 read-write ratio → 20K redirects/sec read
- 5 years → 30B URLs, ~10 TB storage

**Architecture:**
```
[Client] → [Load Balancer] → [API Servers]
                                  │
                     ┌────────────┼────────────┐
                     ↓            ↓            ↓
               [URL Service]  [Analytics]  [User Service]
                     │            │
                     ↓            ↓
              [Database]    [Analytics DB]
                     │
                  [Cache]
```

**Key Decisions:**
- **URL encoding**: Base62 (a-z, A-Z, 0-9) → 6 chars = 62^6 = 56.8B unique URLs
- **ID generation**: Counter-based, pre-generated IDs, UUID, or hash (MD5/SHA) + take first 6 chars
- **Database**: NoSQL (DynamoDB/Cassandra) — key-value, high read throughput
- **Cache**: Redis — cache hot/popular URLs (LRU eviction)
- **Unique constraint**: Check for collision; retry if exists

**URL Generation Approaches:**

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| **MD5 hash + first N chars** | Simple, deterministic | Collision possible |
| **Auto-increment ID → Base62** | No collision | Predictable, single point |
| **Pre-generated keys (KGS)** | Fast, no collision | Service to manage |
| **UUID → Base62** | Distributed, no collision | Too long |
| **Snowflake ID → Base62** | Distributed, ordered | Complex setup |

---

### 21.2 Design Twitter / Social Media Feed

**Requirements:**
- Post tweets (280 chars, images, video)
- Follow/unfollow
- Home timeline (news feed)
- Search tweets
- Trending topics

**Architecture:**
```
[Client] → [CDN for static assets]
         → [Load Balancer] → [API Gateway]
                                  │
              ┌───────────────────┼───────────────────┐
              ↓                   ↓                   ↓
       [Tweet Service]    [Timeline Service]    [User Service]
              │                   │                   │
              ↓                   ↓                   ↓
       [Tweet DB]           [Timeline Cache]    [User DB]
       [Media Store]        [Fan-out Service]   [Graph DB]
              │                   │
              ↓                   ↓
       [Search Index]       [Message Queue]
       (Elasticsearch)         (Kafka)
```

**Fan-out Strategies:**

| Strategy | How | Pros | Cons |
|----------|-----|------|------|
| **Fan-out on Write (Push)** | On tweet, push to all followers' timelines | Read is fast (pre-computed) | Slow for celebrities (millions of followers) |
| **Fan-out on Read (Pull)** | On read, fetch tweets from all followed users | Fast write | Slow read (aggregate at read time) |
| **Hybrid** | Push for normal users, Pull for celebrities | Best of both | Complex implementation |

**Twitter's approach**: Hybrid — fan-out on write for users with < 10K followers; fan-out on read for celebrities.

